/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Draw a circle with TDC at the top and BDC at the bottom, and let the crank turn clockwise.
   - Inlet open period = inlet lead + 180° + inlet lag.
   - Both engines use the same four events in the same order; only the size of the angles and the reasons differ.
   - An SI engine is throttled.
   - A naturally aspirated CI engine has no throttle, but its high compression ratio leaves very little space between the piston crown and the valve heads at TDC, so the valves cannot stand far open there, and the inlet pressure is not above the
   - In a piston-ported engine the piston edge is the valve, so each port opens and closes at angles fixed by the height of the port in the cylinder wall.
   - A piston-controlled inlet port is uncovered by the piston skirt as the piston nears TDC, so its events are symmetric about TDC.
   - Two periods matter.
*/
