/* book Worked example 2: Q: A crankcase-scavenged two-stroke engine has its exhaust port open 60° before BDC, its transfer port open 45° before BDC and its inlet port open 55° before TDC; all ports are piston controlled. Find the open period of each port, the blowdown period, and the time the exhaust stays open after the transfer port closes, at 3000 rev/min.
   1. Piston-controlled ports are symmetric about their dead centre. Exhaust: open 60° before BDC to 60° after BDC = 120°CA.
   2. Transfer: 45° before to 45° after BDC = 90°CA.
   3. Inlet: 55° before to 55° after TDC = 110°CA.
   4. Blowdown, exhaust opening to transfer opening: 60° − 45° = 15°CA.
   5. After the transfer port closes (45° after BDC) the exhaust stays open until 60° after BDC: 15°CA, the same 15° because of the symmetry.
   6. At 3000 rev/min 1° = 0.0556 ms, so each 15° period lasts 0.83 ms and the exhaust is open for 120 × 0.0556 = 6.7 ms.
   Answer: Exhaust 120°CA, transfer 90°CA, inlet 110°CA; blowdown 15°CA and post-transfer exhaust 15°CA, each 0.83 ms at 3000 rev/min. */
