/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The cylinders are numbered from the front (timing-gear or fan end), and cylinder 1 is the reference.
   - n is the number of cylinders and θf is in crank degrees.
   - An inline four has θf = 180°.
   - After cylinder 1 fires, the next TDC belongs to pair 2-3, so either 2 or 3 fires next; then 4 must fire, because 1 has just finished its power stroke and is on exhaust.
   - An inline six (θf = 120°) usually fires 1-5-3-6-2-4, which alternates between the front and rear halves of the engine.
   - If the spark plug leads or injector pipes are fitted in the wrong order, some cylinders fire at the end of exhaust instead of compression, and the engine runs badly or will not start.
   - The layout says where the valves sit.
   - The I-head is described further by where its camshaft is: OHV (camshaft in the block, pushrods and rockers), SOHC (one overhead camshaft in the head) and DOHC (two overhead camshafts, one for the inlet valves and one for the exhaust valves)
   - Each valve opens once per cycle, that is once in two crank revolutions, so the camshaft of a four-stroke engine turns at half crank speed: Ncam = Ncrank/2.
   - A small tappet clearance is left in the train so the valve still closes fully when the stem grows with heat; a hydraulic lifter, an oil-filled plunger, takes up that clearance automatically.
*/
