/* book Worked example: Q: A four-cylinder four-stroke engine with firing order 1-3-4-2 runs at 2400 rev/min. Find the firing interval, the time between two firings, and the stroke each cylinder is on during the first half-turn after cylinder 1 fires.
   1. Firing interval: θf = 720°/4 = 180°.
   2. Speed: 2400/60 = 40 rev/s, so 180° (half a turn) takes 0.5/40 = 0.0125 s = 12.5 ms.
   3. Cylinder 1 fires at 0°, 3 at 180°, 4 at 360°, 2 at 540°. Each cylinder runs power, exhaust, intake, compression in turn, 180° each.
   4. From 0° to 180°: cylinder 1 is on power. Cylinder 3 fires at 180°, so it is on compression. Cylinder 4 fires at 360°, so it is one stroke earlier still, on intake. Cylinder 2 fired at 540° of the previous cycle (−180°), so it is on exhaust.
   5. Check: the four cylinders are on four different strokes, as they must be.
   Answer: θf = 180°, 12.5 ms between firings; in the first half-turn cylinder 1 is on power, 3 on compression, 4 on intake and 2 on exhaust (Figure 1.6.1). */
